<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  legacy28 — targetSdk 28, legacy storage.

  Everything shared is in src/main/AndroidManifest.xml; only the difference is
  here (SPEC §7.2: the two manifests and one di/CaptureModule.kt are the ONLY
  flavour-specific sources).

  On targetSdk 28 the OEM harvest reaches /sdcard/Recordings/... through
  Environment.getExternalStorageDirectory() and plain File objects. That is
  legacy storage, and it is exactly what scoped storage closes — which is why
  this flavour exists and why M0 has to measure rather than assume.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      UC-14: read the handset's own call recordings. READ only — the files are
      never moved, modified or deleted (CONVENTIONS.md §8.3), so
      WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE stays undeclared even though SPEC §7.2 names it.

      ⚠️ This carried `maxSdkVersion="28"` with the note that "above that the
      permission grants nothing and would only add a screen". That reasoning is
      wrong for an app whose targetSdk IS 28: such an app keeps the legacy
      external-storage view on Android 10 and later, and THIS permission is
      what gates it. Capping it meant the flavour built to harvest the OEM
      folder held no storage permission at all on every handset in the fleet —
      the locator would have found nothing on a Samsung and the failure would
      have read as "Samsung cannot record either".

      Found by a static analysis of the shipped APK, 2026-09-11, before the
      first Samsung was tested. It would have sent the measurement the wrong
      way.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE" />

    <!--
      UC-14, the Android 11+ guarantee — and deliberately a SECOND line of
      defence rather than the first.

      Legacy storage should be enough here and costs the agent nothing; where
      an OEM has narrowed it, this is the only route left to another app's
      recordings folder. Which one is in force is a MEASUREMENT, not an
      assumption: `StorageAccessProbe` exercises the folders, and E2 offers the
      All-files screen only when that probe says they are unreachable. So on a
      handset where legacy storage works the agent never meets the alarming
      screen, and on one where it does not they are given the way through
      instead of a silent failure.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE" />

    <!--
      Explicit rather than implied. It defaults to true below targetSdk 29, and
      writing it down is what stops a future target bump from silently removing
      the legacy view that this whole flavour is built on.
    -->
    <application android:requestLegacyExternalStorage="true" />

</manifest>
